      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1670, John Tinker established a trading post with the Nashaway tribe at Nod Brook, marking Groton’s first colonial settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groton%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1828 discovery of a soapstone quarry made Groton home to the nation’s largest soapstone factory, exporting to China.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groton%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Groton hosts two elite boarding schools: Lawrence Academy, founded 1793, and Groton School, founded 1884.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groton%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "First Parish Church, built in 1755 on Main and Lowell, served as the town’s original meeting house and social hub.",
